  "rewrittenText": "Subject: Clear Notes, Better Work\n\nMost teams treat documentation like an administrative chore—a dry obligation shoved into the margins of an afternoon. It shows. When knowledge stays locked behind private conversations and scattered notes, people spend half their working day guessing. We mistake frantic searching for momentum.\n\nIt isn't.\n\nWe need a single workshop for our records, not half a dozen forgotten drawers. When notes are scribbled in haste and left unanchored, critical insights dissolve into thin air. Work should not depend on tribal memory or lucky guesses. A clear, shared record spares everyone the friction of reinventing what has already been solved.\n\nSet aside thirty minutes every Friday to clear the bench and update your project logs. Don't look at modern writing tools as magic engines of speed; look at them as chisels. Sharpen the record so the next person can build without stumbling.",
  "changesExplanation": "The draft was completely stripped of passive corporate throat-clearing ('In today's fast-paced corporate environment', 'it has been observed by several stakeholders') and reconstituted into direct, tactile language. Hollow terms like 'strategic optimization', 'synergistic alignment', and 'collaborative bandwidth' were demolished and replaced with metaphors rooted in manual craftsmanship, carpentry, and physical labor.\n\nThe syntactic cadence was re-engineered to reflect the author's signature burstiness. Expansive observations exploring the frustration of scattered knowledge are paired directly with isolated declarative tripwires ('It isn't.') to halt momentum and command attention. The final paragraph transforms vague administrative requests into an explicit, rhythmic imperative.",
